</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"dccdb20c","external_id":"c709983b-f5d2-4f76-8755-5eb306b94baa","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"d2052bb6-6a47-4153-ba85-c0a5d3b5e385","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.B.","skill_desc":"Percentages","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">There are <math alttext=\"450\"><mn>450</mn>\n</math> tiles in a box. Of these tiles, <math alttext=\"6 percent sign\"><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></math> are black. How many black tiles are in the box?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["27"],"correct_answer":["27"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that <math alttext=\"6 percent sign\"><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the <math alttext=\"450\"><mn>450</mn>\n</math> tiles in a box are black. Therefore, the number of black tiles in the box can be calculated by multiplying the number of tiles in the box by <math alttext=\"StartFraction 6 Over 100 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext=\"450 left parenthesis StartFraction 6 Over 100 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mn>450</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1743430554986,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"dccdb20c","skill_cd":"Q.B.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Percentages","createDate":1743430554986,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"","external_id":"c709983b-f5d2-4f76-8755-5eb306b94baa","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"d2052bb6-6a47-4153-ba85-c0a5d3b5e385","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"type":"spr","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">There are <math alttext=\"450\"><mn>450</mn>\n</math> tiles in a box. Of these tiles, <math alttext=\"6 percent sign\"><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></math> are black. How many black tiles are in the box?</p>","keys":["27"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that <math alttext=\"6 percent sign\"><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the <math alttext=\"450\"><mn>450</mn>\n</math> tiles in a box are black. Therefore, the number of black tiles in the box can be calculated by multiplying the number of tiles in the box by <math alttext=\"StartFraction 6 Over 100 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>, which is equivalent to <math alttext=\"450 left parenthesis StartFraction 6 Over 100 EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mn>450</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn>\n</math>.</p>","externalid":"c709983b-f5d2-4f76-8755-5eb306b94baa","answerOptions":[],"correct_answer":["27"]},"createDate":1743430554986,"updateDate":1743430554986}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430554986, 1743430554986),
    ($SATQ$e03f3477$SATQ$, $SATQ$c69d7c49-d5b4-474f-92d3-d2ef20e9813a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$09a23a47-0b07-4768-b9b9-b75209e1fb07$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.F.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inference from sample statistics and margin of error $SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>A sample consisting of <math alttext="720"><mn>720</mn>
</math> adults who own televisions was selected at random for a study. Based on the sample, it is estimated that <math alttext="32 percent sign"><mn>32</mn>
<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext="3.41 percent sign"><mn>3.41</mn>
<mo>%</mo></math>. Which of the following is the most plausible conclusion about all adults who own televisions?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f9f63ce9-b89f-4128-987d-9efdd772d0ea","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">More than <math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>"},{"id":"09c1ec7f-7a71-48ec-af11-b4ffd38cf4a8","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Between <math alttext=\"28.59 percent sign\"><mn>28.59</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b7de1b71-43cc-4115-8ed2-fc28609d3338","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Since the sample included adults who own televisions and not just those who use their televisions to watch nature shows, no conclusion can be made.</p>"},{"id":"bb5c5503-81d4-455c-9de2-0fefae88c944","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Since the sample did not include all the people who watch nature shows, no conclusion can be made.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["09c1ec7f-7a71-48ec-af11-b4ffd38cf4a8"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. It's given that based on a sample selected at random, it's estimated that <math alttext="32 percent sign"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows, with an associated margin of error of&nbsp;<math alttext="3.41 percent sign"><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Subtracting the margin of error from the estimate and adding the margin of error to the estimate gives an interval of plausible values for the true percentage of adults who own televisions who use their televisions to watch nature shows. This means it's plausible that between <math alttext="32 percent sign minus 3.41 percent sign"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="28.59 percent sign"><mn>28.59</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, and&nbsp;<math alttext="32 percent sign plus 3.41 percent sign"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="35.41 percent sign"><mn>35.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows. Therefore, of the given choices, the most plausible conclusion is that between&nbsp;<math alttext="28.59 percent sign"><mn>28.59</mn><mo>%</mo></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext="35.41 percent sign"><mn>35.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice C is incorrect. To make a plausible conclusion about all adults who own televisions, the sample must be selected at random from all adults who own televisions, not just those who use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Choice D is incorrect. Since the sample was selected at random from all adults who own televisions, a plausible conclusion can be made about all adults who own televisions.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"e03f3477","external_id":"c69d7c49-d5b4-474f-92d3-d2ef20e9813a","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"09a23a47-0b07-4768-b9b9-b75209e1fb07","uId":"3bf0ae78-5461-474e-85ff-5f25065119ab","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.F.","skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>A sample consisting of <math alttext=\"720\"><mn>720</mn>\n</math> adults who own televisions was selected at random for a study. Based on the sample, it is estimated that <math alttext=\"32 percent sign\"><mn>32</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext=\"3.41 percent sign\"><mn>3.41</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math>. Which of the following is the most plausible conclusion about all adults who own televisions?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f9f63ce9-b89f-4128-987d-9efdd772d0ea","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">More than <math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>"},{"id":"09c1ec7f-7a71-48ec-af11-b4ffd38cf4a8","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Between <math alttext=\"28.59 percent sign\"><mn>28.59</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b7de1b71-43cc-4115-8ed2-fc28609d3338","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Since the sample included adults who own televisions and not just those who use their televisions to watch nature shows, no conclusion can be made.</p>"},{"id":"bb5c5503-81d4-455c-9de2-0fefae88c944","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Since the sample did not include all the people who watch nature shows, no conclusion can be made.</p>"}],"keys":["09c1ec7f-7a71-48ec-af11-b4ffd38cf4a8"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It's given that based on a sample selected at random, it's estimated that <math alttext=\"32 percent sign\"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows, with an associated margin of error of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"3.41 percent sign\"><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Subtracting the margin of error from the estimate and adding the margin of error to the estimate gives an interval of plausible values for the true percentage of adults who own televisions who use their televisions to watch nature shows. This means it's plausible that between <math alttext=\"32 percent sign minus 3.41 percent sign\"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"28.59 percent sign\"><mn>28.59</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"32 percent sign plus 3.41 percent sign\"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows. Therefore, of the given choices, the most plausible conclusion is that between&nbsp;<math alttext=\"28.59 percent sign\"><mn>28.59</mn><mo>%</mo></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. To make a plausible conclusion about all adults who own televisions, the sample must be selected at random from all adults who own televisions, not just those who use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. Since the sample was selected at random from all adults who own televisions, a plausible conclusion can be made about all adults who own televisions.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959835,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"e03f3477","skill_cd":"Q.F.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Inference from sample statistics and margin of error ","createDate":1691007959835,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":null,"external_id":"c69d7c49-d5b4-474f-92d3-d2ef20e9813a","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"3bf0ae78-5461-474e-85ff-5f25065119ab","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["09c1ec7f-7a71-48ec-af11-b4ffd38cf4a8"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It's given that based on a sample selected at random, it's estimated that <math alttext=\"32 percent sign\"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows, with an associated margin of error of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"3.41 percent sign\"><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Subtracting the margin of error from the estimate and adding the margin of error to the estimate gives an interval of plausible values for the true percentage of adults who own televisions who use their televisions to watch nature shows. This means it's plausible that between <math alttext=\"32 percent sign minus 3.41 percent sign\"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"28.59 percent sign\"><mn>28.59</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"32 percent sign plus 3.41 percent sign\"><mn>32</mn><mo>%</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math>, of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows. Therefore, of the given choices, the most plausible conclusion is that between&nbsp;<math alttext=\"28.59 percent sign\"><mn>28.59</mn><mo>%</mo></math> and&nbsp;<math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect. To make a plausible conclusion about all adults who own televisions, the sample must be selected at random from all adults who own televisions, not just those who use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect. Since the sample was selected at random from all adults who own televisions, a plausible conclusion can be made about all adults who own televisions.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<p>A sample consisting of <math alttext=\"720\"><mn>720</mn>\n</math> adults who own televisions was selected at random for a study. Based on the sample, it is estimated that <math alttext=\"32 percent sign\"><mn>32</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows, with an associated margin of error of <math alttext=\"3.41 percent sign\"><mn>3.41</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math>. Which of the following is the most plausible conclusion about all adults who own televisions?</p>","externalid":"c69d7c49-d5b4-474f-92d3-d2ef20e9813a","templateid":"96954a6e-c89b-41e1-9b32-1fa440ac07f2","vaultid":"09a23a47-0b07-4768-b9b9-b75209e1fb07","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"f9f63ce9-b89f-4128-987d-9efdd772d0ea","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">More than <math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.</p>"},{"id":"09c1ec7f-7a71-48ec-af11-b4ffd38cf4a8","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Between <math alttext=\"28.59 percent sign\"><mn>28.59</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> and <math alttext=\"35.41 percent sign\"><mn>35.41</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> of all adults who own televisions use their televisions to watch nature shows.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"b7de1b71-43cc-4115-8ed2-fc28609d3338","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Since the sample included adults who own televisions and not just those who use their televisions to watch nature shows, no conclusion can be made.</p>"},{"id":"bb5c5503-81d4-455c-9de2-0fefae88c944","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Since the sample did not include all the people who watch nature shows, no conclusion can be made.</p>"}],"correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1691007959835,"updateDate":1691007959835}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959835, 1691007959835),
